   </div></details></nav></div></header><script>(function(){var d=document.querySelector(".mnav");if(!d)return;document.addEventListener("click",function(e){if(d.open&&!d.contains(e.target))d.open=false});document.addEventListener("keydown",function(e){if(e.key==="Escape"&&d.open){d.open=false;d.querySelector("summary").focus()}})})();</script><main><nav class="crumb w" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <span>Practice charting at home</span></nav><section class="hero w"><h1>Practice Charting at Home on Family and Friends</h1><p class="lead">Take vital signs on someone you live with who says it's OK, then chart them in the same Epic-style EHR you use for school cases. Nickname only, with normal ranges, the AHA's blood pressure steps and a sample note below.</p><div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="/app#ehr" data-cta="hc-hero">Start charting free</a><a class="btn alt" href="#how" data-cta="hc-how">How it works</a></div></section><div class="w"><style>
.vx-val{margin:34px 0}.vx-val h2{margin-top:0}
.vx-cmp{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,230px),1fr));gap:12px;margin:16px 0}
.vx-cmp>div{background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:14px 16px;display:grid;gap:4px;align-content:start}
.vx-cmp b{font:400 20px/1.2 var(--fh)}.vx-cmp .amt{font:700 24px/1.2 var(--fb);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;color:var(--ink)}.vx-cmp small{color:var(--muted);font-size:14px;line-height:1.4}
.vx-cmp .vx-us{background:var(--navy);border-color:var(--navy);color:var(--navy-ink)}.vx-cmp .vx-us .amt,.vx-cmp .vx-us b{color:var(--navy-ink)}.vx-cmp .vx-us small{color:var(--navy-ink);opacity:.85}
.vx-calc{background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--line);border-top:4px solid var(--gold);border-radius:10px;padding:16px 18px;margin:18px 0}
.vx-calc h3{margin:0 0 8px}.vx-calc .vc-in{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px 16px;align-items:center}.vx-calc label{font-weight:700}
.vx-calc input[type=range]{flex:1 1 220px;min-width:0;accent-color:var(--accent)}.vx-calc input[type=number]{width:96px;font:17px var(--fb);padding:7px 9px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;background:var(--bg);color:var(--ink)}
.vx-calc fieldset{border:0;padding:0;margin:10px 0 0;display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px 18px}.vx-calc legend{font-size:14.5px;color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:4px;padding:0}.vx-calc fieldset label{font-weight:400;display:inline-flex;gap:6px;align-items:center}
.vx-out{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,200px),1fr));gap:10px;margin:14px 0 4px}.vx-out>div{background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px}
.vx-out span{display:block;font-size:14px;color:var(--muted)}.vx-out output{display:block;font:700 22px/1.25 var(--fb);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.vx-out small{color:var(--muted)}
.vx-note{font-size:14px;color:var(--muted);margin:6px 0 0}
.vx-ps{max-width:520px}.vx-ps td:nth-child(n+3),.vx-ps th:nth-child(n+3){text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;white-space:nowrap}
.vx-scale{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(100%,220px),1fr));gap:8px 18px;list-style:none;padding:0;margin:12px 0}.vx-scale li{line-height:1.35}.vx-scale b{color:var(--accent)}
.vx-fn{font-size:14px}.vx-fn td,.vx-fn th{padding:6px 8px}.vx-fn a{overflow-wrap:anywhere}
.vx-strip{background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--line);border-left:5px solid var(--gold);border-radius:10px;padding:16px 18px;margin:30px 0}
.vx-strip h2{font-size:clamp(22px,3.2vw,28px);margin:0 0 6px}.vx-strip ul{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;list-style:none;padding:0;margin:10px 0}
.vx-strip li{background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;padding:4px 12px;font-size:14.5px}.vx-strip li b{font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.vx-strip p{margin:6px 0}.hc-card{max-width:420px;margin:8px 0 24px;border:1px solid var(--line,#d9e2e8);border-radius:14px;background:#fff;box-shadow:0 10px 30px rgba(16,58,79,.12);padding:16px 18px;font-size:15px}
.hc-top{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px}.hc-top b{display:block;font-size:17px}.hc-top small{color:var(--muted,#5b6b75);font-size:14px}.hc-av{width:44px;height:44px;border-radius:50%;background:#1d5670;color:#fff;display:grid;place-items:center;font-weight:700}.hc-tag{margin-left:auto;background:#fdf3dc;color:#7a5400;border-radius:999px;padding:4px 10px;font-size:14px}
.hc-v{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:8px;margin:14px 0 10px}.hc-v div{background:#f3f7f9;border-radius:10px;padding:8px}.hc-v small{display:block;color:var(--muted,#5b6b75);font-size:14px}.hc-v b{font-size:18px}
.hc-f{margin:0 0 12px;padding:8px 10px;border-left:4px solid #e0a82e;background:#fffaf0;font-size:14.5px}.hc-t{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px}.hc-t span{font-size:14px;border:1px solid var(--line,#d9e2e8);border-radius:999px;padding:4px 10px;color:#1d5670}
.hc-steps{counter-reset:s;list-style:none;padding:0}.hc-steps li{counter-increment:s;position:relative;padding:0 0 14px 44px}.hc-steps li:before{content:counter(s);position:absolute;left:0;top:0;width:30px;height:30px;border-radius:50%;background:#1d5670;color:#fff;display:grid;place-items:center;font-weight:700}
.hc-ex{background:#f3f7f9;border-radius:10px;padding:12px 14px;font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;font-size:14.5px;white-space:pre-wrap}</style>
    <div class="hc-card" role="img" aria-label="Example of a home practice patient in the Vexcura EHR: Mom, parent, 50s, with a blood pressure of 128/78 flagged as AHA Elevated">
   <div class="hc-top"><span class="hc-av" aria-hidden="true">M</span><div><b>Mom</b><small>Parent · 50s · Home practice</small></div><span class="hc-tag">Home</span></div>
   <div class="hc-v"><div><small>BP</small><b>128/78</b></div><div><small>HR</small><b>72</b></div><div><small>RR</small><b>16</b></div><div><small>SpO₂</small><b>98%</b></div></div>
   <p class="hc-f">128/78 is AHA Elevated (120–129 and under 80).</p>
   <div class="hc-t"><span>Summary</span><span>Flowsheet</span><span>Notes</span><span>Care plan</span><span>Handoff</span></div></div>
    <h2 id="how">How to practice charting at home</h2>
    <ol class="hc-steps">
     <li><b>Ask first.</b> Practice only on someone who says it's OK. For a child, their parent or guardian says OK too.</li>
     <li><b>Add them as a home practice patient.</b> In Vexcura's EHR, choose <i>Add patient</i>, then <i>Home practice patient</i>. Pick a nickname like “Mom”, the relationship and an age range.</li>
     <li><b>Take a full set of vital signs.</b> Temperature, pulse, respirations, blood pressure and SpO₂ if you have a pulse oximeter, in the same order every time.</li>
     <li><b>Chart it like clinicals.</b> Enter the vitals in the flowsheet, then write a short note. Vexcura checks each reading against common ranges as you save it.</li>
     <li><b>Practice the rest.</b> A head-to-toe assessment, a care plan and an SBAR handoff, all on the same practice patient.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2 id="ranges">Normal vital signs for adults at rest</h2>
    <div class="tw"><table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Vital sign</th><th>Usual adult range</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody>
     <tr><td><b>Temperature</b></td><td data-l="Usual adult range">97.8 °F to 99.1 °F (36.5 °C to 37.3 °C)</td><td data-l="Source">Cleveland Clinic<sup><a href="#hc-src-2">3</a></sup></td></tr>
     <tr><td><b>Pulse</b></td><td data-l="Usual adult range">60 to 100 beats per minute</td><td data-l="Source">Cleveland Clinic<sup><a href="#hc-src-2">3</a></sup></td></tr>
     <tr><td><b>Respirations</b></td><td data-l="Usual adult range">12 to 18 breaths per minute</td><td data-l="Source">Cleveland Clinic<sup><a href="#hc-src-2">3</a></sup></td></tr>
     <tr><td><b>Blood pressure</b></td><td data-l="Usual adult range">Normal is under 120/80 mm Hg</td><td data-l="Source">American Heart Association<sup><a href="#hc-src-0">1</a></sup></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="src" style="color:var(--muted);font-size:14.5px">Children's ranges depend on age, so compare a child's readings with your program's pediatric chart. Textbooks differ slightly; on exams, use the ranges your program teaches.</p>
    <h3 id="bp-categories">Blood pressure categories (American Heart Association)</h3>
    <div class="tw"><table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Systolic (top)</th><th>Diastolic (bottom)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
     <tr><td><b>Normal</b></td><td data-l="Systolic">Less than 120</td><td data-l="Diastolic">and less than 80</td></tr>
     <tr><td><b>Elevated</b></td><td data-l="Systolic">120 to 129</td><td data-l="Diastolic">and less than 80</td></tr>
     <tr><td><b>Stage 1 hypertension</b></td><td data-l="Systolic">130 to 139</td><td data-l="Diastolic">or 80 to 89</td></tr>
     <tr><td><b>Stage 2 hypertension</b></td><td data-l="Systolic">140 or higher</td><td data-l="Diastolic">or 90 or higher</td></tr>
     <tr><td><b>Hypertensive crisis</b></td><td data-l="Systolic">Higher than 180</td><td data-l="Diastolic">and/or higher than 120</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="src" style="color:var(--muted);font-size:14.5px">From the American Heart Association<sup><a href="#hc-src-0">1</a></sup>. One reading isn't a diagnosis. Above 180/120 with chest pain, shortness of breath, back pain, numbness or weakness, a change in vision or trouble speaking, the AHA says call 911.</p>
    <h2 id="bp-at-home">How to take a blood pressure at home</h2>
    <p>The American Heart Association's home steps<sup><a href="#hc-src-1">2</a></sup> are a good checklist to say out loud while you practice:</p>
    <ul><li>No smoking, caffeine or exercise for 30 minutes before.</li><li>Sit with the back supported, feet flat on the floor and legs uncrossed, and rest quietly for 5 minutes.</li><li>Rest the arm on a flat surface at heart level.</li><li>Put the cuff on bare skin just above the bend of the elbow, not over clothing, and use the right cuff size.</li><li>Don't talk during the reading.</li></ul>
    <h2 id="document">How to document vital signs</h2>
    <p>Chart what you measured, how you measured it and anything that affects the number. An example note for a home practice patient:</p>
    <p class="hc-ex">1830 Seated, R arm, adult cuff, after 5 min rest: BP 128/78, HR 72 regular, RR 16 unlabored, T 98.4 °F oral, SpO₂ 98% on room air. Denies pain. BP in AHA Elevated range; recheck in 5 min.</p>
    <p>Put the numbers in the flowsheet and keep the note short. Write what you saw, not a diagnosis.</p>
    <h2 id="privacy">Privacy rules for practicing on family</h2>
    <div class="feat"><div><b>Their OK first</b>Vexcura won't save a home practice patient until you confirm they said it's OK, and for a child, that a parent or guardian did too.</div><div><b>Nickname only</b>A relationship nickname like “Mom” or “Roommate”. Full names and initials are blocked.</div><div><b>An age range, not a birthday</b>Age ranges from “Under 12” to “90+”. No birthdays, addresses or photos.</div><div><b>No medications or diagnoses</b>Medication pages are off for home practice patients, and mental-health and substance screeners are hidden.</div></div>
    <h2 id="in-vexcura">Practice charting in Vexcura's EHR</h2>
    <p>Home practice patients live in the same Epic-style EHR as the case library: a patient summary, flowsheets, notes, a care plan and an SBAR handoff. As you save vitals, Vexcura shows which readings are in range and which aren't, using the AHA blood pressure categories, and shows a safety message if a reading is in a dangerous range. It's a practice tool, not medical advice.</p>
    <p><a class="btn" href="/app#ehr" data-cta="hc-start">Start charting free</a> <a class="btn alt" href="/academic-ehr-for-nursing-students" data-cta="hc-ehr">See the whole EHR</a></p>
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><h3>Can nursing students practice charting at home?</h3><p>Yes. Take a set of vital signs or a head-to-toe assessment on someone you live with who says it's OK, then document it the way you would in clinicals. In Vexcura you add them as a home practice patient in the same EHR you use for school cases, and chart vitals, notes, a care plan and an SBAR handoff.</p><h3>Is it OK to practice on family members?</h3><p>Only with their OK, and for a child, with a parent or guardian's OK too. Vexcura asks you to confirm that before it saves the patient, and it only keeps a nickname like “Mom”, a relationship and an age range. Don't enter their real medications, diagnoses, birthday or address.</p><h3>What vital signs should I practice?</h3><p>Temperature, pulse, respirations, blood pressure and, if you have a pulse oximeter, oxygen saturation. Practice the full set together, in the same order every time, and write down position, arm and cuff size with each blood pressure.</p><h3>What are normal vital signs for adults?</h3><p>For a healthy adult at rest, Cleveland Clinic lists a temperature of 97.8 °F to 99.1 °F, a pulse of 60 to 100 beats per minute and 12 to 18 breaths per minute. The American Heart Association calls a blood pressure under 120/80 normal. Use your program's textbook ranges on exams, since sources differ slightly.</p><h3>What if I get a high blood pressure reading on a family member?</h3><p>Recheck after they've rested quietly for 5 minutes with good technique, since one reading isn't a diagnosis. If readings stay high they should talk with their own doctor. The American Heart Association says a reading higher than 180/120 with chest pain, shortness of breath, back pain, numbness or weakness, a change in vision or trouble speaking means call 911.</p><h3>Is home charting practice free in Vexcura?</h3><p>Yes. Every nursing student can create a free account, and home practice patients live in the same Epic-style EHR as the case library. They're saved to your account, so they're there on any device you sign in on.</p><h3>Does a home practice patient count as a real medical record?</h3><p>No. It's a practice chart with a nickname and an age range, no names, birthdays, addresses, medications or diagnoses, so it isn't a record of anyone's care. Treat what family members tell you as private anyway, the same way you would at clinicals.</p></div>
    <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2><ol class="os-src"><li id="hc-src-0">American Heart Association, <a href="https://www.heart.org/en/health-topics/high-blood-pressure/understanding-blood-pressure-readings" rel="nofollow noopener">Understanding blood pressure readings</a> (checked November 17, 2026).</li><li id="hc-src-1">American Heart Association, <a href="https://www.heart.org/-/media/GRFW-Files/Know-Your-Risk/measuring-your-blood-pressure-at-home-English.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener">Measuring your blood pressure at home (PDF)</a> (checked November 17, 2026).</li><li id="hc-src-2">Cleveland Clinic, <a href="https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/10881-vital-signs" rel="nofollow noopener">Vital signs</a> (checked November 17, 2026).</li></ol>
    <p class="src" style="color:var(--muted);font-size:14.5px">Vexcura is a practice tool for nursing students, not medical advice. It isn't affiliated with or endorsed by the American Heart Association, Cleveland Clinic or Epic Systems Corporation; “Epic-style” describes a familiar layout.</p><h2>Related</h2><ul class="rel"><li><a href="/academic-ehr-for-nursing-students">Epic-style academic EHR</a></li><li><a href="/for-nursing-students">Vexcura for nursing students</a></li><li><a href="/nclex-practice-questions">Free NCLEX practice questions by category</a></li><li><a href="/free-nclex-practice-exam">Full adaptive NCLEX-RN practice exam</a></li><li><a href="/nclex-lab-values">NCLEX lab values</a></li></ul></div><section class="band vxs-band"><div class="vxs-bg" data-vxs-i="0" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="vxs-sh" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="w"><h2>Free for every nursing student</h2><p>Create an account and get the full adaptive NCLEX-RN practice exam, 1,220 cited questions, 8 NGN case studies, the Epic-style academic EHR and the simulations, with or without a school. Nursing programs license Vexcura for their cohorts and faculty, and every plan includes everything.</p><div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="/app#signup" data-cta="band">Create your free account</a><a class="btn alt" href="/for-nursing-schools">For nursing schools</a></div></div></section></main><footer class="foot mktf"><div class="w">
